WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.870
So this is another place where you might find
C02 everyday at work or

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.670
maybe even at home in the kitchen.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.839
We use it in fire extinguishers. Now if we
shake this bottle we can hear

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:12.889
that there's some liquid slopping around inside
and that's liquefied C02.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:18.740
So C02 is useful as an extinguisher for fires
because it doesn't erm sustain

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.250
combustion it's non-flammable. So if there
is a fire, we squirt C02 which

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:29.670
is quite dense; it blankets over the fire,
excludes oxygen and puts the

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:37.269
fire out. So let's see some more gaseous C02.

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:42.199
Carbon dioxide is a really interesting molecule.
It's very simple. It has

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:46.469
carbon here with two oxygen atoms one on each
side so it’s just three

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:47.980
atoms in a row.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.220
Today what we're going to do is we're going
to explore the three states

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.719
of matter looking at a single molecule and
we’re going to use C02. So at

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:59.320
standard temperature and pressure, C02's a
gas and actually I’m

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.609
breathing it out so here we go we'll make
some C02 [breathes out].

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:10.330
The molecule has many interesting properties.
One of them is that most

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.780
gases, when you compress them, will liquefy
and form a liquid and then if you

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:22.829
cool the liquid down it will freeze. What
is interesting about Carbon

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:28.950
Dioxide is that you cannot get the liquid
at atmospheric pressure. So you

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:35.130
can get all sorts of little containers that
contain liquid C02 but if you open

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.780
them, release the pressure, the C02 converts
into a solid, so called dry

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:47.190
ice, which is very cold but goes straight
from the solid to the gas without erm

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.200
melting.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.469
So hopefully today we’re going to look at
C02 as a gas, C02 as a liquid

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:56.570
and C02 as a solid and perhaps we’ll look
at some reactions as well.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:01.549
There are double bonds between the carbon
and the oxygen because

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:05.909
each oxygen normally wants to be bonded to
two atoms but here you

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:10.959
can see it's only bonded to one carbon so
it has a double bond, two

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:12.930
bonds together.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:17.840
So this is where we're going to get our C02
from, this rather large

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.519
cylinder. So on the cylinder we have a device
called a regulator which

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:28.459
you can see here. Now what that does is it
limits the pressure of the gas

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.370
that we get out into our pipe. So this is
really for safety because the

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.060
pressure inside the cylinder is a lot higher
than the pressure that we

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.010
require in our reaction or inside our balloon.
So here we have a balloon,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:47.110
a rather strange balloon. So now you can see
we’ve collected a balloon

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.980
full of carbon dioxide and as you can see
it’s a rather nice balloon, the same

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.210
as any other balloon, what do you say about
a balloon and C02? So C02

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.560
is a gas [laughs]

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:09.050
C02 is one of my favourite molecules because
C02 as a liquid or a highly

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:16.660
compressed gas will dissolve chemicals just
in the same way as petrol or

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:22.130
other organic liquids will dissolve them and
so you can use carbon

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:26.860
dioxide as a solvent for chemical reactions
and this highly compressed

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.750
gas, the so called super critical fluid, is
something that both I and Pete

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.370
Licence, who does lots of our demonstrations,
we've done a lot of

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.489
research on this.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:41.790
Most people when they think of C02 they think
of it as a gas but if you

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.430
take that gas and you pressurise that gas
and you squeeze it, if you put

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.989
it into a bottle perhaps or a container and
you squeeze it, the gas

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:52.840
molecules are brought closer and closer and
closer together until

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.140
eventually they will condense and form a liquid
phase. Now what we’ve

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.799
done with our experiment, is we’ve taken
the C02 and we've connected

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:04.659
it to a special piece of apparatus which you
can see is made out of things

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:09.569
like stainless steel and technical instrumentation.
But the key thing about

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:14.910
this apparatus is that unlike a normal stainless
steel vessel we have a

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.549
window inside this vessel which is able to
maintain pressure, it’s able to

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:26.100
take the stresses because of the pressure
trying to erm push against the

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.100
atmosphere.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:31.590
And it's really good for chemical reactions
and again by using carbon

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:38.340
dioxide as a solvent, you can avoid having
to use products made from

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:45.130
petroleum which might otherwise use up and
deplete the petroleum. With

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.491
carbon dioxide, we have more than we know
what to do with from

00:04:48.491 --> 00:04:55.530
various industrial processes. So it is available
really quite widely.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.990
So the window that you’re looking through
in this experiment is sapphire.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:01.990
That must be expensive

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.440
Hell yes, they're about, the front window
in this cell is approximately

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.500
£800 and the rear window, the one which moves,
is about £1400. So

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.580
quite expensive but absolutely critical for
this experiment.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:24.460
And if, as is proposed, C02 is captured from
power stations this could be

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:31.300
huge amounts of C02 and if you can use that
for chemical reactions, for

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.699
extracting materials. One of the main commercial
applications of high

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:41.850
pressure carbon dioxide at the moment is for
decaffeinating coffee.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:47.380
Many years ago, coffee was decaffeinated using
dry cleaning fluids which

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:51.840
can leave all sorts of residues in the coffee
beans, people may not want

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:59.349
to drink the coffee. But caffeine is soluble
in C02 and most of the other

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:03.400
components of coffee beans are not so you
can extract the caffeine

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.880
from green coffee beans so you get decaffeinated
coffee and the C02

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.110
leaves no residues at all and then by reducing
the pressure or washing

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:18.099
the C02 with water you can get the caffeine
out and you can sell it to

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:21.610
people to put in their soft drinks.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:26.880
Now, Neil's been really active and he's ordered
in a special piece of C02

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:37.940
for us which he's going to get out of the
box now. So erm what we

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.960
thought we'd do today is perhaps a little
bit of erm ice sculpting. So

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.100
we're going to cut the C02 in half and make
a reaction vessel. Ok? And

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.200
then we're going to do a nice chemical reaction
with it. So here Neil’s

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.200
cutting the C02 now with a saw. Now this is
a typical saw that you might

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:05.590
find in a hardware shop or a do it yourself
shop. So one of the

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:08.780
advantages of sawing this material is that
you don't have to clean up the

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:13.530
saw dust because it just evaporates.

